<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Society of Suriname</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1683–1795</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Geoctroyeerde Sociëteit van Suriname</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> was a chartered company formed in 1683 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a>, the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornelis-van-aerssen-van-sommelsdijck">Cornelis van Aerssen van Sommelsdijck</a>, each holding a third, to own and govern the colony of Suriname. Its directors in Amsterdam appointed the governor, who ruled with a council chosen from planters' nominees. It governed the colony until 1795, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> state took it over.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/society-of-suriname.jpg" alt="Society of Suriname" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was T. Tichelaar at Dutch Wikipedia. (Original text: nog onbekend) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1918handenvoetboogdoelenBU183302.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
